% Aggregates, ordering, grouping and joins: the edges (T05, review of 2026-09-29).
%
% Every expectation is derived from spec/SPEC.md (7.3 aggregates, 7.4 structure,
% 3.4 identity, 6.4 limits), not from a host's output. Where the spec leaves a
% question open -- the order of mixed numeric-looking and other text, the
% meaning of same-named join binders, BUCKET's binder spelling -- the case
% asserts only what holds under EVERY consistent answer: idempotence, that a
% permutation of the input cannot change a sort, TOP == TAKE(SORT), and that a
% join does not depend on whether the optimiser found an equi-join.

% Iteration is over a SNAPSHOT of the source's children (SPEC 7.3)

### name: agg.snapshot.append-to-list-map
--- note
An aggregate visits the elements its source had when it started. A key the body adds is not visited, and a child the body overwrites is still visited as it was; the element itself, as the binder names it, is the same value (3.4), so a mutation INSIDE an element is seen. The report cases crashed JS (TypeError/heap), Python, PHP and C++ (use after free) and the hosts disagreed on the live/snapshot answer.
--- source
A = (1, 2); COUNT(MAP(A, A[COUNT(A) + 1] = 0))
--- expect
num 2
===
