### name: agg.snapshot.replacing-the-source-variable-changes-nothing
--- note
Rebinding the variable is not a mutation of the value already being visited (3.4).
--- source
A = LIST(1, 2, 3); MAP(A, (A = LIST(9, 9, 9); _)) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "1,2,3"
===
### name: agg.snapshot.mutation-inside-an-element-is-seen
--- note
The binder names the element (3.4): a mutation inside a later element is visible when it is visited.
--- source
A = LIST(RECORD("x", 1), RECORD("x", 2)); MAP(A, (A[2]["x"] = 50; _["x"])) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "1,50"
===

% A scalar is a one-element list; NULL is an empty one (SPEC 7.3)

### name: sort.scalar-is-one-element-list
--- note
A first argument with no children is a one-element list when it has a scalar and an empty list when it is NONE (7.3), for every aggregate. The sorts, TOP* and BUCKET treated a scalar as empty in most hosts (lisp/go for TOP, five hosts for BUCKET); C++ alone followed the text.
--- source
SORT("str")
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"str"}
===
### name: sort-desc.scalar-is-one-element-list
--- note
A first argument with no children is a one-element list when it has a scalar and an empty list when it is NONE (7.3), for every aggregate. The sorts, TOP* and BUCKET treated a scalar as empty in most hosts (lisp/go for TOP, five hosts for BUCKET); C++ alone followed the text.
--- source
SORT_DESC(5)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"5"}
===
