### name: bucket.empty-text-key
--- note
The empty string is a key like any other.
--- source
BUCKET(LIST("a", ""), _) .> INDEXES .> JOIN("|")
--- expect
text "a|"
===

% Direction and count are always evaluated and checked (SPEC 7.4)

### name: sort-by.bad-direction-on-an-empty-list
--- note
SORT_BY's direction and TOP's count are arguments like any other: they are evaluated, and rejected when wrong, whether or not the list has anything to sort. PHP did; JS, Python, Lisp and C++ skipped them for an empty list, which made a rule's validity depend on its data.
--- source
SORT_BY(LIST(), _ + 0, "X")
--- expect
error E_BAD_ARG at 1:24
===
### name: sort-by.bad-direction-on-a-null-source
--- note
SORT_BY's direction and TOP's count are arguments like any other: they are evaluated, and rejected when wrong, whether or not the list has anything to sort. PHP did; JS, Python, Lisp and C++ skipped them for an empty list, which made a rule's validity depend on its data.
--- source
SORT_BY(NULL, _, "UP")
--- expect
error E_BAD_ARG at 1:18
===
### name: sort-by.non-text-direction-on-an-empty-list
--- note
SORT_BY's direction and TOP's count are arguments like any other: they are evaluated, and rejected when wrong, whether or not the list has anything to sort. PHP did; JS, Python, Lisp and C++ skipped them for an empty list, which made a rule's validity depend on its data.
--- source
SORT_BY(LIST(), _ + 0, 1)
--- expect
error E_BAD_ARG at 1:24
===
### name: sort-by.good-direction-on-an-empty-list
--- note
SORT_BY's direction and TOP's count are arguments like any other: they are evaluated, and rejected when wrong, whether or not the list has anything to sort. PHP did; JS, Python, Lisp and C++ skipped them for an empty list, which made a rule's validity depend on its data.
--- source
COUNT(SORT_BY(LIST(), _, "DESC"))
--- expect
num 0
===
### name: sort-by.direction-error-precedes-key-error-on-a-non-empty-list
--- note
SORT_BY's direction and TOP's count are arguments like any other: they are evaluated, and rejected when wrong, whether or not the list has anything to sort. PHP did; JS, Python, Lisp and C++ skipped them for an empty list, which made a rule's validity depend on its data. The key is evaluated per element, the direction once, before any of them.
--- source
SORT_BY(LIST(1), 1 / 0, "UP")
--- expect
error E_BAD_ARG at 1:25
===
### name: top-by.bad-direction-on-an-empty-list
--- note
SORT_BY's direction and TOP's count are arguments like any other: they are evaluated, and rejected when wrong, whether or not the list has anything to sort. PHP did; JS, Python, Lisp and C++ skipped them for an empty list, which made a rule's validity depend on its data.
--- source
TOP_BY(LIST(), _, "UP", 1)
--- expect
error E_BAD_ARG at 1:19
===
### name: top.count-error-on-an-empty-list
--- note
SORT_BY's direction and TOP's count are arguments like any other: they are evaluated, and rejected when wrong, whether or not the list has anything to sort. PHP did; JS, Python, Lisp and C++ skipped them for an empty list, which made a rule's validity depend on its data.
--- source
TOP(LIST(), 1 / 0)
--- expect
error E_DIV_ZERO at 1:15
===
### name: top.negative-count-on-an-empty-list
--- note
SORT_BY's direction and TOP's count are arguments like any other: they are evaluated, and rejected when wrong, whether or not the list has anything to sort. PHP did; JS, Python, Lisp and C++ skipped them for an empty list, which made a rule's validity depend on its data.
--- source
TOP(LIST(), -1)
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:13
===
### name: top.fractional-count-on-an-empty-list
--- note
SORT_BY's direction and TOP's count are arguments like any other: they are evaluated, and rejected when wrong, whether or not the list has anything to sort. PHP did; JS, Python, Lisp and C++ skipped them for an empty list, which made a rule's validity depend on its data.
--- source
TOP(LIST(), 1.5)
--- expect
error E_NOT_INT at 1:13
===
### name: top-desc.count-error-on-a-null-source
--- note
SORT_BY's direction and TOP's count are arguments like any other: they are evaluated, and rejected when wrong, whether or not the list has anything to sort. PHP did; JS, Python, Lisp and C++ skipped them for an empty list, which made a rule's validity depend on its data.
--- source
TOP_DESC(NULL, 1 / 0)
--- expect
error E_DIV_ZERO at 1:18
===

% TOP(n) is TAKE(SORT, n) for every n, including huge ones (SPEC 7.4)

### name: top-by.equals-take-of-sort-by.n-0
--- note
TOP_BY is 'the first n of SORT_BY, as one step': same rows, same keys, same tie order (the sorts are stable), for n below, at and past the size.
--- source
L = LIST(RECORD("k", 3, "id", "a"), RECORD("k", 1, "id", "b"), RECORD("k", 3, "id", "c"), RECORD("k", 2, "id", "d"), RECORD("k", 1, "id", "e")); TOP_BY(L, _["k"], 0) EQL TAKE(SORT_BY(L, _["k"]), 0)
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: top-by-desc.equals-take-of-sort-by-desc.n-0
--- note
The same for the descending spelling, where a tie keeps its input order too.
--- source
L = LIST(RECORD("k", 3, "id", "a"), RECORD("k", 1, "id", "b"), RECORD("k", 3, "id", "c"), RECORD("k", 2, "id", "d"), RECORD("k", 1, "id", "e")); TOP_BY(L, _["k"], "DESC", 0) EQL TAKE(SORT_BY(L, _["k"], "DESC"), 0)
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
