--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: sort-by.desc-ties-keep-input-order
--- note
Every sort is stable, the descending ones included: equal keys keep their order.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("k", 3, "id", "a"), RECORD("k", 1, "id", "b"), RECORD("k", 3, "id", "c"), RECORD("k", 2, "id", "d"), RECORD("k", 1, "id", "e")) .> SORT_BY(_["k"], "DESC") .> MAP(_["id"]) .> JOIN("")
--- expect
text "acdbe"
===
### name: sort-by.asc-ties-keep-input-order
--- note
Ascending: ties in input order.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("k", 3, "id", "a"), RECORD("k", 1, "id", "b"), RECORD("k", 3, "id", "c"), RECORD("k", 2, "id", "d"), RECORD("k", 1, "id", "e")) .> SORT_BY(_["k"]) .> MAP(_["id"]) .> JOIN("")
--- expect
text "bedac"
===
### name: top.huge-count-is-the-whole-list
--- note
A count is a size, not an allocation: 10^30 asks for everything there is. Lisp allocated an N-element heap up front (a TYPE-ERROR at 10^30, heap exhaustion at 10^9).
--- source
LIST(3, 1, 2) .> TOP(1000000000000000000000000000000) .> COUNT()
--- expect
num 3
===
### name: top.count-one-billion-is-the-whole-list
--- note
The same at a count that fits a machine word.
--- source
COUNT(TOP(LIST(3, 1, 2), 1000000000))
--- expect
num 3
===
### name: top-by.huge-count-is-the-whole-list
--- note
The keyed spelling.
--- source
LIST(3, 1, 2) .> TOP_BY(_, 1000000000000000000000000000000) .> COUNT()
--- expect
num 3
===
### name: top-desc.huge-count-is-the-whole-list
--- note
The descending spelling.
--- source
LIST(3, 1, 2) .> TOP_DESC(1000000000000000000000000000000) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "3,2,1"
===

% The sort order: one total order over every kind (SPEC 7.3)

### name: sort.total-order-of-every-kind
--- note
The order (7.3): by kind NULL < BOOL (FALSE < TRUE) < numeric-looking text, by exact decimal value < every other TEXT, bytewise < BIN, bytewise. Values that compare equal keep their input order; a descending sort reverses unequal ranks but keeps ties in input order. `"10"`, `"9"` and `"1a"` used to sort five different ways on six hosts. `" 2"` (leading space) and `"1e3"` (no exponent form) are not numbers (4); `"-0"`, `"0"` tie at zero and `"007"`, `"7"` at seven, in input order.
--- source
SORT(LIST("10", "9", "1a", "", " 2", "-0", "1e3", "007", "7", "0", FROM_HEX("00ff"), TRUE, FALSE, NULL))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-, "2"=FALSE, "3"=TRUE, "4"=t"-0", "5"=t"0", "6"=t"007", "7"=t"7", "8"=t"9", "9"=t"10", "10"=t"", "11"=t" 2", "12"=t"1a", "13"=t"1e3", "14"=b00ff}
===
### name: sort-desc.total-order-of-every-kind
--- note
The order (7.3): by kind NULL < BOOL (FALSE < TRUE) < numeric-looking text, by exact decimal value < every other TEXT, bytewise < BIN, bytewise. Values that compare equal keep their input order; a descending sort reverses unequal ranks but keeps ties in input order. `"10"`, `"9"` and `"1a"` used to sort five different ways on six hosts. Descending reverses the unequal ranks; the tied pairs (`"-0"`, `"0"`) and (`"007"`, `"7"`) stay in input order.
--- source
SORT_DESC(LIST("10", "9", "1a", "", " 2", "-0", "1e3", "007", "7", "0", FROM_HEX("00ff"), TRUE, FALSE, NULL))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=b00ff, "2"=t"1e3", "3"=t"1a", "4"=t" 2", "5"=t"", "6"=t"10", "7"=t"9", "8"=t"007", "9"=t"7", "10"=t"-0", "11"=t"0", "12"=TRUE, "13"=FALSE, "14"=-}
===
### name: sort.numbers-and-numeric-text-are-one-rank
--- note
The order (7.3): by kind NULL < BOOL (FALSE < TRUE) < numeric-looking text, by exact decimal value < every other TEXT, bytewise < BIN, bytewise. Values that compare equal keep their input order; a descending sort reverses unequal ranks but keeps ties in input order. `"10"`, `"9"` and `"1a"` used to sort five different ways on six hosts. A number and the text spelling it are the same value.
--- source
SORT(LIST("10", 9, "9.5", 10)) .> MAP(_) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "9,9.5,10,10"
===
### name: sort.numeric-text-before-other-text
--- note
The order (7.3): by kind NULL < BOOL (FALSE < TRUE) < numeric-looking text, by exact decimal value < every other TEXT, bytewise < BIN, bytewise. Values that compare equal keep their input order; a descending sort reverses unequal ranks but keeps ties in input order. `"10"`, `"9"` and `"1a"` used to sort five different ways on six hosts.
--- source
LIST("10", "9", "1a") .> SORT() .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "9,10,1a"
===
### name: sort-desc.other-text-before-numeric-text
--- note
The order (7.3): by kind NULL < BOOL (FALSE < TRUE) < numeric-looking text, by exact decimal value < every other TEXT, bytewise < BIN, bytewise. Values that compare equal keep their input order; a descending sort reverses unequal ranks but keeps ties in input order. `"10"`, `"9"` and `"1a"` used to sort five different ways on six hosts.
--- source
LIST("10", "9", "1a") .> SORT_DESC() .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "1a,10,9"
===
### name: sort.numeric-text-by-value-not-bytes
--- note
The order (7.3): by kind NULL < BOOL (FALSE < TRUE) < numeric-looking text, by exact decimal value < every other TEXT, bytewise < BIN, bytewise. Values that compare equal keep their input order; a descending sort reverses unequal ranks but keeps ties in input order. `"10"`, `"9"` and `"1a"` used to sort five different ways on six hosts.
--- source
LIST("100", "20", "3", "-5", "2.50", "2.5") .> SORT() .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "-5,2.50,2.5,3,20,100"
===
### name: sort.other-text-is-bytewise
--- note
The order (7.3): by kind NULL < BOOL (FALSE < TRUE) < numeric-looking text, by exact decimal value < every other TEXT, bytewise < BIN, bytewise. Values that compare equal keep their input order; a descending sort reverses unequal ranks but keeps ties in input order. `"10"`, `"9"` and `"1a"` used to sort five different ways on six hosts. Code-point order above U+FFFF included.
--- source
LIST("b", "B", "a1", "\u{1F600}", "\u{FF5E}", "") .> SORT() .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text ",B,a1,b,～,😀"
===
### name: sort-by.key-ranks-like-the-elements
--- note
The order (7.3): by kind NULL < BOOL (FALSE < TRUE) < numeric-looking text, by exact decimal value < every other TEXT, bytewise < BIN, bytewise. Values that compare equal keep their input order; a descending sort reverses unequal ranks but keeps ties in input order. `"10"`, `"9"` and `"1a"` used to sort five different ways on six hosts.
--- source
LIST("x", "10", "9") .> SORT_BY(_) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "9,10,x"
===
### name: sort-by.desc-mixed-keys-keep-ties-in-input-order
--- note
The order (7.3): by kind NULL < BOOL (FALSE < TRUE) < numeric-looking text, by exact decimal value < every other TEXT, bytewise < BIN, bytewise. Values that compare equal keep their input order; a descending sort reverses unequal ranks but keeps ties in input order. `"10"`, `"9"` and `"1a"` used to sort five different ways on six hosts.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("k", "1", "n", "a"), RECORD("k", "a", "n", "b"), RECORD("k", "1.0", "n", "c"), RECORD("k", "1", "n", "d")) .> SORT_BY(_["k"], "DESC") .> MAP(_["n"]) .> JOIN("")
--- expect
text "bacd"
===
### name: sort.every-permutation-has-the-same-rank-sequence
--- note
The order (7.3): by kind NULL < BOOL (FALSE < TRUE) < numeric-looking text, by exact decimal value < every other TEXT, bytewise < BIN, bytewise. Values that compare equal keep their input order; a descending sort reverses unequal ranks but keeps ties in input order. `"10"`, `"9"` and `"1a"` used to sort five different ways on six hosts. Eight permutations of the fourteen values, compared by the sequence of kinds and values with the numeric ties spelled by value: no permutation changes the answer.
--- source
X = SORT(LIST("10", "9", "1a", "", " 2", "-0", "1e3", "007", "7", "0", FROM_HEX("00ff"), TRUE, FALSE, NULL)) .> MAP(IF(_ EQL NULL, "N", IF(_ EQL FALSE, "F", IF(_ EQL TRUE, "T", IF(_ EQL FROM_HEX("00ff"), "B", IF(ISNUM(_), "n" & (_ + 0), "s" & _)))))) .> JOIN(","); (SORT(LIST(NULL, FALSE, TRUE, FROM_HEX("00ff"), "0", "7", "007", "1e3", "-0", " 2", "", "1a", "9", "10")) .> MAP(IF(_ EQL NULL, "N", IF(_ EQL FALSE, "F", IF(_ EQL TRUE, "T", IF(_ EQL FROM_HEX("00ff"), "B", IF(ISNUM(_), "n" & (_ + 0), "s" & _)))))) .> JOIN(",")) $== X AND (SORT(LIST("1e3", "7", FALSE, "-0", "0", "1a", "10", "9", "", NULL, " 2", TRUE, "007", FROM_HEX("00ff"))) .> MAP(IF(_ EQL NULL, "N", IF(_ EQL FALSE, "F", IF(_ EQL TRUE, "T", IF(_ EQL FROM_HEX("00ff"), "B", IF(ISNUM(_), "n" & (_ + 0), "s" & _)))))) .> JOIN(",")) $== X AND (SORT(LIST("0", "7", TRUE, "10", " 2", FALSE, "-0", "9", "007", FROM_HEX("00ff"), "", "1a", "1e3", NULL)) .> MAP(IF(_ EQL NULL, "N", IF(_ EQL FALSE, "F", IF(_ EQL TRUE, "T", IF(_ EQL FROM_HEX("00ff"), "B", IF(ISNUM(_), "n" & (_ + 0), "s" & _)))))) .> JOIN(",")) $== X AND (SORT(LIST(" 2", "9", "0", "1a", "", "7", NULL, FROM_HEX("00ff"), "-0", "007", "10", TRUE, "1e3", FALSE)) .> MAP(IF(_ EQL NULL, "N", IF(_ EQL FALSE, "F", IF(_ EQL TRUE, "T", IF(_ EQL FROM_HEX("00ff"), "B", IF(ISNUM(_), "n" & (_ + 0), "s" & _)))))) .> JOIN(",")) $== X AND (SORT(LIST("-0", FALSE, TRUE, " 2", "10", NULL, "007", "1e3", "9", "1a", "7", "", FROM_HEX("00ff"), "0")) .> MAP(IF(_ EQL NULL, "N", IF(_ EQL FALSE, "F", IF(_ EQL TRUE, "T", IF(_ EQL FROM_HEX("00ff"), "B", IF(ISNUM(_), "n" & (_ + 0), "s" & _)))))) .> JOIN(",")) $== X AND (SORT(LIST(NULL, "-0", "007", "1e3", FROM_HEX("00ff"), "9", "", "0", FALSE, "10", "7", " 2", "1a", TRUE)) .> MAP(IF(_ EQL NULL, "N", IF(_ EQL FALSE, "F", IF(_ EQL TRUE, "T", IF(_ EQL FROM_HEX("00ff"), "B", IF(ISNUM(_), "n" & (_ + 0), "s" & _)))))) .> JOIN(",")) $== X AND (SORT(LIST("1e3", "0", FALSE, FROM_HEX("00ff"), "7", "", "9", " 2", "1a", TRUE, "10", "-0", NULL, "007")) .> MAP(IF(_ EQL NULL, "N", IF(_ EQL FALSE, "F", IF(_ EQL TRUE, "T", IF(_ EQL FROM_HEX("00ff"), "B", IF(ISNUM(_), "n" & (_ + 0), "s" & _)))))) .> JOIN(",")) $== X
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: sort.idempotent-every-kind
--- note
SORT(SORT(x)) is SORT(x).
--- source
S = SORT(LIST("10", "9", "1a", "", " 2", "-0", "1e3", "007", "7", "0", FROM_HEX("00ff"), TRUE, FALSE, NULL)); S EQL SORT(S)
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: sort-desc.idempotent-every-kind
