TOP(L, n) is the first n of SORT(L).
--- source
L = LIST("10", "9", "1a", "", " 2", "-0", "1e3", "007", "7", "0", FROM_HEX("00ff"), TRUE, FALSE, NULL); TOP(L, 15) EQL TAKE(SORT(L), 15)
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: top-desc.every-kind-equals-take-of-sort-desc.n-15
--- note
The descending spelling.
--- source
L = LIST("10", "9", "1a", "", " 2", "-0", "1e3", "007", "7", "0", FROM_HEX("00ff"), TRUE, FALSE, NULL); TOP_DESC(L, 15) EQL TAKE(SORT_DESC(L), 15)
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: sort-by.every-kind-key-equals-plain-sort
--- note
SORT_BY with the element as its key is SORT.
--- source
L = LIST("10", "9", "1a", "", " 2", "-0", "1e3", "007", "7", "0", FROM_HEX("00ff"), TRUE, FALSE, NULL); SORT_BY(L, _) EQL SORT(L)
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: sort.twelve-element-list-from-the-report
--- note
The order (7.3): by kind NULL < BOOL (FALSE < TRUE) < numeric-looking text, by exact decimal value < every other TEXT, bytewise < BIN, bytewise. Values that compare equal keep their input order; a descending sort reverses unequal ranks but keeps ties in input order. `"10"`, `"9"` and `"1a"` used to sort five different ways on six hosts.
--- source
LIST("1a", "x", "100", "1b", "9", "10", "2", "5", "30", "3b", "4", "33") .> SORT() .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "2,4,5,9,10,30,33,100,1a,1b,3b,x"
===
### name: sort-desc.twelve-element-list-from-the-report
--- note
The order (7.3): by kind NULL < BOOL (FALSE < TRUE) < numeric-looking text, by exact decimal value < every other TEXT, bytewise < BIN, bytewise. Values that compare equal keep their input order; a descending sort reverses unequal ranks but keeps ties in input order. `"10"`, `"9"` and `"1a"` used to sort five different ways on six hosts.
--- source
LIST("1a", "x", "100", "1b", "9", "10", "2", "5", "30", "3b", "4", "33") .> SORT_DESC() .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "x,3b,1b,1a,100,33,30,10,9,5,4,2"
===
### name: sort.twelve-element-list-take-four-is-the-prefix
--- note
The order (7.3): by kind NULL < BOOL (FALSE < TRUE) < numeric-looking text, by exact decimal value < every other TEXT, bytewise < BIN, bytewise. Values that compare equal keep their input order; a descending sort reverses unequal ranks but keeps ties in input order. `"10"`, `"9"` and `"1a"` used to sort five different ways on six hosts. The fused TOP and the sort-then-TAKE agree.
--- source
L = LIST("1a", "x", "100", "1b", "9", "10", "2", "5", "30", "3b", "4", "33"); S = SORT(L); JOIN(TAKE(S, 4), ",") $== JOIN(TOP(L, 4), ",")
--- expect
bool TRUE
===

% BUCKET keys: index keys in two arguments, identity in three (SPEC 7.3)

### name: bucket.two-arg-same-key-text-different-structure
--- note
In the two-argument spelling the group key is an INDEX KEY (3.3): the key's scalar, verbatim. A value that carries a scalar and children is still that scalar, so two of them with different children and the same text are one group, and every member is kept. All six hosts kept only the last.
--- setup
A = "x"; A["k"] = 1; B = "x"; B["k"] = 2
--- source
BUCKET(LIST(A, B, "x"), _)
--- expect
tree -{"x"=-{"1"=t"x"{"k"=t"1"}, "2"=t"x"{"k"=t"2"}, "3"=t"x"}}
===
### name: bucket.two-arg-same-key-text-keeps-every-row
--- note
In the two-argument spelling the group key is an INDEX KEY (3.3): the key's scalar, verbatim. A value that carries a scalar and children is still that scalar, so two of them with different children and the same text are one group, and every member is kept. All six hosts kept only the last.
--- setup
A = "x"; A["k"] = 1; B = "x"; B["k"] = 2
--- source
R = LIST(A, "x", A) .> BUCKET(_); JOIN(LIST(COUNT(R), COUNT(R["x"])), ",")
--- expect
text "1,3"
===
### name: bucket.two-arg-same-key-text-first-appearance-order
--- note
In the two-argument spelling the group key is an INDEX KEY (3.3): the key's scalar, verbatim. A value that carries a scalar and children is still that scalar, so two of them with different children and the same text are one group, and every member is kept. All six hosts kept only the last.
--- setup
A = "x"; A["k"] = 1; B = "x"; B["k"] = 2
--- source
R = LIST("a", A, "b", B, "x") .> BUCKET(_); INDEXES(R) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "a,x,b"
===
### name: bucket.two-arg-bare-record-spelling
--- note
In the two-argument spelling the group key is an INDEX KEY (3.3): the key's scalar, verbatim. A value that carries a scalar and children is still that scalar, so two of them with different children and the same text are one group, and every member is kept. All six hosts kept only the last.
--- source
x = "a"; x["k"] = 1; y = "a"; y["k"] = 2; BUCKET(LIST(x, y), _)
--- expect
tree -{"a"=-{"1"=t"a"{"k"=t"1"}, "2"=t"a"{"k"=t"2"}}}
===
### name: bucket.three-arg-same-text-different-structure-is-three-groups
--- note
In the three-argument spelling the group key is compared by IDENTITY (3.4) and may be any value: equal spelling with different structure is a different key.
--- setup
A = "x"; A["k"] = 1; B = "x"; B["k"] = 2
--- source
BUCKET(LIST(A, B, "x"), _, COUNT(_)) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "1,1,1"
===
### name: bucket.three-arg-identity-groups-repeats
--- note
In the three-argument spelling the group key is compared by IDENTITY (3.4) and may be any value: equal spelling with different structure is a different key.
--- setup
A = "x"; A["k"] = 1; B = "x"; B["k"] = 2
--- source
LIST(A, "x", A) .> BUCKET(_, COUNT(_)) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "2,1"
===
### name: bucket.three-arg-numbers-are-not-normalised
--- note
In the three-argument spelling the group key is compared by IDENTITY (3.4) and may be any value: equal spelling with different structure is a different key. `"1"` and `"1.0"` are different keys (5.4).
--- source
BUCKET(LIST("1", "1.0", "1"), _, COUNT(_)) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "2,1"
===
### name: bucket.two-arg-numbers-are-not-normalised
--- note
Index keys are verbatim (3.3): `"1"` and `"1.0"` are two keys.
--- source
BUCKET(LIST("1", "1.0", "1"), _) .> INDEXES .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "1,1.0"
===
### name: bucket.warm-cache-text-key
--- note
A decimal cache warmed on a value must not change which group it falls in (Lisp split equal text keys into separate groups once `X * 1` had been evaluated).
--- source
X = "5"; Y = X * 1; JOIN(BUCKET(LIST(X, "5", 5), _, COUNT(_)), ",")
--- expect
text "3"
===
### name: bucket.warm-cache-leading-zero-key
--- note
A decimal cache warmed on a value must not change which group it falls in (Lisp split equal text keys into separate groups once `X * 1` had been evaluated).
--- source
X = "01"; Y = X + 0; JOIN(BUCKET(LIST(X, "01", "1"), _, COUNT(_)), ",")
--- expect
text "2,1"
===
### name: bucket.warm-cache-trailing-zero-key
--- note
A decimal cache warmed on a value must not change which group it falls in (Lisp split equal text keys into separate groups once `X * 1` had been evaluated).
--- source
X = "1.0"; Y = X + 0; JOIN(BUCKET(LIST(X, "1.0"), _, COUNT(_)), ",")
--- expect
text "2"
===
### name: bucket.warm-cache-record-key-three-arg
--- note
A decimal cache warmed on a value must not change which group it falls in (Lisp split equal text keys into separate groups once `X * 1` had been evaluated).
--- source
R = LIST(RECORD("k", "1"), RECORD("k", "1"), RECORD("k", "2")); x = ANY(R, _["k"] > 0); BUCKET(R, _["k"], COUNT(_))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"2", "2"=t"1"}
===
### name: bucket.warm-cache-record-key-two-arg-keeps-the-rows
--- note
A decimal cache warmed on a value must not change which group it falls in (Lisp split equal text keys into separate groups once `X * 1` had been evaluated).
--- source
R = LIST(RECORD("k", "1"), RECORD("k", "1"), RECORD("k", "2")); x = ANY(R, _["k"] > 0); BUCKET(R, _["k"]) .> MAP(COUNT(_)) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "2,1"
===
### name: bucket.empty-record-key-K
--- note
`_K` is the element's key. An element whose record key is the empty string has `_K` equal to the empty string, not an invented counter.
--- source
BUCKET(RECORD("", 5, "x", 6), IF(_K $== "", "empty", "other"))
--- expect
tree -{"empty"=-{"1"=t"5"}, "other"=-{"1"=t"6"}}
===
### name: bucket.empty-record-key-K-as-key
--- note
`_K` is the element's key. An element whose record key is the empty string has `_K` equal to the empty string, not an invented counter.
--- source
BUCKET(RECORD("", 5, "x", 6, "y", 7), _K) .> INDEXES .> JOIN("|")
--- expect
text "|x|y"
===
### name: map.empty-record-key-K-control
--- note
The control: MAP already read the empty key correctly everywhere.
--- source
MAP(RECORD("", 5, "x", 6), _K) .> JOIN("|")
--- expect
text "|x"
===
### name: bucket.three-arg-bool-key-groups-by-identity
--- note
In the three-argument spelling the group key is any value compared by identity (3.4), `_K` is that value, BIN and BOOL included; in the two-argument spelling those keys are E_NOT_TEXT.
--- source
LIST(1, 2, 3, 4) .> BUCKET(_ > 2, COUNT(_)) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "2,2"
===
### name: bucket.three-arg-bool-key-is-the-value
--- note
In the three-argument spelling the group key is any value compared by identity (3.4), `_K` is that value, BIN and BOOL included; in the two-argument spelling those keys are E_NOT_TEXT.
--- source
LIST(1, 2, 3) .> BUCKET(_ > 1, _K)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=FALSE, "2"=TRUE}
===
### name: bucket.three-arg-bin-key-groups-by-identity
--- note
In the three-argument spelling the group key is any value compared by identity (3.4), `_K` is that value, BIN and BOOL included; in the two-argument spelling those keys are E_NOT_TEXT.
--- source
LIST("a", "b", "a") .> BUCKET(TO_UTF8(_), COUNT(_)) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "2,1"
===
### name: bucket.three-arg-bin-key-is-the-value
--- note
In the three-argument spelling the group key is any value compared by identity (3.4), `_K` is that value, BIN and BOOL included; in the two-argument spelling those keys are E_NOT_TEXT.
--- source
BUCKET(LIST("a", "b"), TO_UTF8(_), _K) EQL LIST(TO_UTF8("a"), TO_UTF8("b"))
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: bucket.three-arg-bin-and-text-of-the-same-bytes-are-two-groups
--- note
In the three-argument spelling the group key is any value compared by identity (3.4), `_K` is that value, BIN and BOOL included; in the two-argument spelling those keys are E_NOT_TEXT. Identity separates kinds: the BIN `61` and the text `"a"` are different keys.
--- source
LIST("a", TO_UTF8("a")) .> BUCKET(_, COUNT(_)) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "1,1"
===
### name: bucket.three-arg-null-key-is-a-group
--- note
In the three-argument spelling the group key is any value compared by identity (3.4), `_K` is that value, BIN and BOOL included; in the two-argument spelling those keys are E_NOT_TEXT. A NULL key is a key.
--- source
COUNT(BUCKET(LIST(1, 2), NULL, _))
--- expect
num 1
===
### name: bucket.three-arg-list-key-groups-by-all-fields
--- note
In the three-argument spelling the group key is any value compared by identity (3.4), `_K` is that value, BIN and BOOL included; in the two-argument spelling those keys are E_NOT_TEXT.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("a", 1, "b", 1), RECORD("a", 1, "b", 2), RECORD("a", 1, "b", 1)) .> BUCKET(LIST(_["a"], _["b"]), COUNT(_)) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "2,1"
===
### name: bucket.two-arg-null-key
--- note
A NULL group key is E_NULL, reported at the key expression (7.3).
--- source
BUCKET(LIST(1), NULL)
--- expect
error E_NULL at 1:17
===
### name: bucket.two-arg-bool-key
--- note
A boolean key is E_NOT_TEXT at the key expression.
--- source
BUCKET(LIST(1), TRUE)
--- expect
error E_NOT_TEXT at 1:17
===
### name: bucket.two-arg-list-key
--- note
A list or record key is E_NOT_TEXT at the key expression.
--- source
BUCKET(LIST(1), LIST(1, 2))
--- expect
error E_NOT_TEXT at 1:17
===
### name: bucket.two-arg-projection-equivalence
--- note
`BUCKET(list, key) .> MAP(proj)` is the three-argument spelling for every key the two-argument one accepts (7.3).
--- source
L = LIST(RECORD("c", "b", "n", 1), RECORD("c", "a", "n", 2), RECORD("c", "b", "n", 3)); BUCKET(L, _["c"]) .> MAP(SUM(_, _["n"])) EQL BUCKET(L, _["c"], SUM(_, _["n"]))
--- expect
bool TRUE
===

% SELECT_COLS with a repeated column name

### name: select-cols.repeated-column-uniform-rows
--- note
A record has one field per name: naming a column twice keeps it once. Python's fast path built duplicate keys (COUNT 3).
--- source
R = SELECT_COLS(LIST(RECORD("a", 1, "b", 2)), "a", "a", "b"); COUNT(R[1])
--- expect
num 2
===
### name: select-cols.repeated-column-last
--- note
The repeat need not be adjacent.
--- source
R = SELECT_COLS(LIST(RECORD("a", 1, "b", 2)), "a", "b", "a"); COUNT(R[1])
--- expect
num 2
===
### name: select-cols.repeated-column-values
--- note
The repeated column keeps its value.
--- source
R = SELECT_COLS(LIST(RECORD("a", 1, "b", 2)), "a", "b", "a"); R[1]["a"] + R[1]["b"]
--- expect
num 3
===
### name: select-cols.repeated-column-non-uniform-rows
--- note
Rows of different shapes take the same rule.
--- source
SELECT_COLS(LIST(RECORD("a", 1, "b", 2), RECORD("b", 3, "a", 4)), "a", "b", "a") .> MAP(COUNT(_)) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "2,2"
===
### name: select-cols.repeated-column-indexes-do-not-repeat
--- note
No key appears twice in a projected row.
--- source
SELECT_COLS(LIST(RECORD("a", 1, "b", 2)), "b", "a", "b") .> MAP(COUNT(INDEXES(_))) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "2"
===

% LINK: what the optimiser finds must not change the answer (SPEC 7.4)

### name: link.equi-numeric-key-beyond-int64-not-equal
--- note
A `LINK` whose predicate is one comparison reading one binder on each side may be matched by key; every other predicate is evaluated per pair. Appending `AND TRUE` turns the first into the second and must never change the rows. 10^23 and 10^24 are different numbers: C++ joined them (a fast key that overflowed).
--- setup
A = LIST(RECORD("k", "100000000000000000000000.0")); B = LIST(RECORD("k", "1000000000000000000000000"))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(A, B, a["k"] == b["k"]))
--- expect
num 0
===
### name: link.equi-numeric-key-beyond-int64-not-equal-general-path
--- note
A `LINK` whose predicate is one comparison reading one binder on each side may be matched by key; every other predicate is evaluated per pair. Appending `AND TRUE` turns the first into the second and must never change the rows.
--- setup
A = LIST(RECORD("k", "100000000000000000000000.0")); B = LIST(RECORD("k", "1000000000000000000000000"))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(A, B, a["k"] == b["k"] AND TRUE))
--- expect
num 0
===
### name: link.equi-numeric-key-trailing-zero-spelling
--- note
A `LINK` whose predicate is one comparison reading one binder on each side may be matched by key; every other predicate is evaluated per pair. Appending `AND TRUE` turns the first into the second and must never change the rows. The same number, `.50` and `.5`, beyond int64: C++ missed the match.
--- setup
A = LIST(RECORD("k", "12345678901234567890.50")); B = LIST(RECORD("k", "12345678901234567890.5"))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(A, B, a["k"] == b["k"]))
--- expect
num 1
===
### name: link.equi-numeric-key-trailing-zero-spelling-reversed
--- note
A `LINK` whose predicate is one comparison reading one binder on each side may be matched by key; every other predicate is evaluated per pair. Appending `AND TRUE` turns the first into the second and must never change the rows. The other direction.
--- setup
A = LIST(RECORD("k", "12345678901234567890.50")); B = LIST(RECORD("k", "12345678901234567890.5"))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(B, A, b["k"] == a["k"]))
--- expect
num 1
===
### name: link.equi-numeric-key-trailing-zero-spelling-general-path
--- note
A `LINK` whose predicate is one comparison reading one binder on each side may be matched by key; every other predicate is evaluated per pair. Appending `AND TRUE` turns the first into the second and must never change the rows.
--- setup
A = LIST(RECORD("k", "12345678901234567890.50")); B = LIST(RECORD("k", "12345678901234567890.5"))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(A, B, a["k"] == b["k"] AND TRUE))
--- expect
num 1
===
### name: link.equi-numeric-key-integer-with-point-zero
--- note
A `LINK` whose predicate is one comparison reading one binder on each side may be matched by key; every other predicate is evaluated per pair. Appending `AND TRUE` turns the first into the second and must never change the rows.
--- setup
A = LIST(RECORD("k", "123456789012345678900.0")); B = LIST(RECORD("k", "123456789012345678900"))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(A, B, a["k"] == b["k"]))
--- expect
num 1
===
### name: link.equi-numeric-key-number-literals-five-argument-form
--- note
A `LINK` whose predicate is one comparison reading one binder on each side may be matched by key; every other predicate is evaluated per pair. Appending `AND TRUE` turns the first into the second and must never change the rows. Number literals, not text, on both sides.
--- setup
X = LIST(RECORD("id", 12345678901234567890.0)); Y = LIST(RECORD("id", 12345678901234567890, "name", "ann"))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(X, Y, L, R, L["id"] == R["id"]))
--- expect
num 1
===
### name: link.equi-numeric-key-signed-zero-with-scale
--- note
A `LINK` whose predicate is one comparison reading one binder on each side may be matched by key; every other predicate is evaluated per pair. Appending `AND TRUE` turns the first into the second and must never change the rows. Negative zero and a scaled zero are one number (`-0 == 0.00`): PHP's key kept the scale of a zero and missed the match.
--- setup
A = LIST(RECORD("k", -0)); B = LIST(RECORD("k", 0.00))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(A, B, a["k"] == b["k"]))
--- expect
num 1
===
### name: link.equi-numeric-key-signed-zero-with-scale-general-path
--- note
A `LINK` whose predicate is one comparison reading one binder on each side may be matched by key; every other predicate is evaluated per pair. Appending `AND TRUE` turns the first into the second and must never change the rows.
--- setup
A = LIST(RECORD("k", -0)); B = LIST(RECORD("k", 0.00))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(A, B, a["k"] == b["k"] AND TRUE))
--- expect
num 1
===
### name: link.equi-numeric-key-text-zeros
--- note
A `LINK` whose predicate is one comparison reading one binder on each side may be matched by key; every other predicate is evaluated per pair. Appending `AND TRUE` turns the first into the second and must never change the rows. Zero written as text, signed and scaled, meets every other spelling of zero, number or text.
--- setup
A = LIST(RECORD("k", "-0"), RECORD("k", "0.0"), RECORD("k", "-0.000")); B = LIST(RECORD("k", 0), RECORD("k", 0.00), RECORD("k", "0"))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(A, B, a["k"] == b["k"]))
--- expect
num 9
===
### name: link.equi-numeric-key-text-zeros-general-path
--- note
A `LINK` whose predicate is one comparison reading one binder on each side may be matched by key; every other predicate is evaluated per pair. Appending `AND TRUE` turns the first into the second and must never change the rows.
--- setup
A = LIST(RECORD("k", "-0"), RECORD("k", "0.0"), RECORD("k", "-0.000")); B = LIST(RECORD("k", 0), RECORD("k", 0.00), RECORD("k", "0"))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(A, B, a["k"] == b["k"] AND TRUE))
--- expect
num 9
===
### name: link.equi-numeric-key-small-scales-and-text
--- note
A `LINK` whose predicate is one comparison reading one binder on each side may be matched by key; every other predicate is evaluated per pair. Appending `AND TRUE` turns the first into the second and must never change the rows. Within int64: trailing fraction zeros, text against number, and a negative fraction are matched by value; `1.1` and `1.01` stay apart.
--- setup
A = LIST(RECORD("k", "1.10"), RECORD("k", -1.50), RECORD("k", "2"), RECORD("k", 1.01)); B = LIST(RECORD("k", 1.1), RECORD("k", "-1.5"), RECORD("k", 2.000))
--- source
LINK(A, B, _1["k"] == _2["k"]) .> MAP(_["_1"]["k"] & "=" & _["_2"]["k"]) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "1.10=1.1,-1.50=-1.5,2=2.000"
===
### name: link.equi-numeric-key-small-scales-and-text-general-path
--- note
A `LINK` whose predicate is one comparison reading one binder on each side may be matched by key; every other predicate is evaluated per pair. Appending `AND TRUE` turns the first into the second and must never change the rows.
--- setup
A = LIST(RECORD("k", "1.10"), RECORD("k", -1.50), RECORD("k", "2"), RECORD("k", 1.01)); B = LIST(RECORD("k", 1.1), RECORD("k", "-1.5"), RECORD("k", 2.000))
--- source
LINK(A, B, _1["k"] == _2["k"] AND TRUE) .> MAP(_["_1"]["k"] & "=" & _["_2"]["k"]) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "1.10=1.1,-1.50=-1.5,2=2.000"
===
### name: link.equi-text-key-case-is-significant
--- note
A `LINK` whose predicate is one comparison reading one binder on each side may be matched by key; every other predicate is evaluated per pair. Appending `AND TRUE` turns the first into the second and must never change the rows. `$==` compares bytes.
--- setup
A = LIST(RECORD("k", "Ab")); B = LIST(RECORD("k", "aB"))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(A, B, a["k"] $== b["k"]))
--- expect
num 0
===
### name: link.equi-key-over-digit-cap
--- note
An equi-join key is compared as `==` compares it, so a numeric key past the digit cap raises E_RANGE (6.4) on both paths; the fast path returned 0 or a match instead.
--- setup
BIG = PADL("9", 1000005, "9")
--- source
LINK(LIST(RECORD("a", "1")), LIST(RECORD("b", BIG)), _1["a"] == _2["b"]) .> COUNT()
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:67
===
### name: link.equi-key-over-digit-cap-general-path
--- note
An equi-join key is compared as `==` compares it, so a numeric key past the digit cap raises E_RANGE (6.4) on both paths; the fast path returned 0 or a match instead.
--- setup
BIG = PADL("9", 1000005, "9")
--- source
LINK(LIST(RECORD("a", "1")), LIST(RECORD("b", BIG)), _1["a"] == _2["b"] AND TRUE) .> COUNT()
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:67
===
### name: link.equi-key-over-digit-cap-both-sides
--- note
An equi-join key is compared as `==` compares it, so a numeric key past the digit cap raises E_RANGE (6.4) on both paths; the fast path returned 0 or a match instead. Equal giant keys on both sides are still E_RANGE, not a match.
--- setup
O = LIST(RECORD("k", REPEAT("9", 1000001))); C = LIST(RECORD("k", REPEAT("9", 1000001)))
--- source
LINK(O, C, _1["k"] == _2["k"]) .> COUNT()
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:14
===
### name: link.same-binder-name-both-sides
--- note
The right binder shadows the left (7.4): when both binders are spelled alike, every occurrence names the RIGHT element, so the predicate reads one element per pair and the join is decided by the general path. A fast path must not extract a key from a comparison whose two sides read the same binder name, and appending `AND TRUE` must not change the answer. `x["k"] == x["k"]` is true for every pair: 2 x 2.
--- setup
P = LIST(RECORD("k", 1), RECORD("k", 2)); Q = LIST(RECORD("k", 1), RECORD("k", 3))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(P, Q, x, x, x["k"] == x["k"]))
--- expect
num 4
===
### name: link.same-binder-name-both-sides-general-path
--- note
The right binder shadows the left (7.4): when both binders are spelled alike, every occurrence names the RIGHT element, so the predicate reads one element per pair and the join is decided by the general path. A fast path must not extract a key from a comparison whose two sides read the same binder name, and appending `AND TRUE` must not change the answer.
--- setup
P = LIST(RECORD("k", 1), RECORD("k", 2)); Q = LIST(RECORD("k", 1), RECORD("k", 3))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(P, Q, x, x, x["k"] == x["k"] AND TRUE))
--- expect
num 4
===
### name: link.same-relation-name-both-sides
--- note
The right binder shadows the left (7.4): when both binders are spelled alike, every occurrence names the RIGHT element, so the predicate reads one element per pair and the join is decided by the general path. A fast path must not extract a key from a comparison whose two sides read the same binder name, and appending `AND TRUE` must not change the answer.
--- setup
X = LIST(RECORD("k", 1), RECORD("k", 2))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(X, X, x["k"] == x["k"]))
--- expect
num 4
===
### name: link.same-relation-name-both-sides-general-path
--- note
The right binder shadows the left (7.4): when both binders are spelled alike, every occurrence names the RIGHT element, so the predicate reads one element per pair and the join is decided by the general path. A fast path must not extract a key from a comparison whose two sides read the same binder name, and appending `AND TRUE` must not change the answer.
--- setup
X = LIST(RECORD("k", 1), RECORD("k", 2))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(X, X, x["k"] == x["k"] AND TRUE))
--- expect
num 4
===
### name: link.self-join-same-name-reads-only-the-right-element
--- note
The right binder shadows the left (7.4): when both binders are spelled alike, every occurrence names the RIGHT element, so the predicate reads one element per pair and the join is decided by the general path. A fast path must not extract a key from a comparison whose two sides read the same binder name, and appending `AND TRUE` must not change the answer. `T["id"] == T["mgr"]` is then a test on the right element alone, and no element has id equal to mgr.
--- setup
T = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "mgr", 2), RECORD("id", 2, "mgr", 1), RECORD("id", 3, "mgr", 9))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(T, T, T["id"] == T["mgr"]))
--- expect
num 0
===
### name: link.self-join-same-name-general-path
--- note
The right binder shadows the left (7.4): when both binders are spelled alike, every occurrence names the RIGHT element, so the predicate reads one element per pair and the join is decided by the general path. A fast path must not extract a key from a comparison whose two sides read the same binder name, and appending `AND TRUE` must not change the answer.
--- setup
T = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "mgr", 2), RECORD("id", 2, "mgr", 1), RECORD("id", 3, "mgr", 9))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(T, T, T["id"] == T["mgr"] AND TRUE))
--- expect
num 0
===
### name: link.self-join-same-name-when-a-row-matches-itself
--- note
The right binder shadows the left (7.4): when both binders are spelled alike, every occurrence names the RIGHT element, so the predicate reads one element per pair and the join is decided by the general path. A fast path must not extract a key from a comparison whose two sides read the same binder name, and appending `AND TRUE` must not change the answer. One element has id equal to mgr, so it matches every left element: 3.
--- setup
T = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "mgr", 2), RECORD("id", 2, "mgr", 2), RECORD("id", 3, "mgr", 9))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(T, T, T["id"] == T["mgr"]))
--- expect
num 3
===
### name: link.distinct-binder-names-still-use-the-key
--- note
The control: with two different binder names the predicate is a keyed comparison and finds the self-join pairs.
--- setup
T = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "mgr", 2), RECORD("id", 2, "mgr", 1), RECORD("id", 3, "mgr", 9))
--- source
COUNT(LINK(T, T, A, B, A["id"] == B["mgr"]))
--- expect
num 2
===
### name: link.comma-list-operand-reading-both-binders
--- note
A side of the comparison that reads BOTH binders (through a `,` list or a `;` sequence) is not one-sided, so the join is evaluated per pair and no binder is unbound. JS and Lisp classified it as one-sided and raised a spurious E_UNDEF_VAR.
--- source
LINK(LIST(RECORD("k", 1)), LIST(RECORD("k", 1)), (_1["k"], _2["k"]) == _1["k"]) .> COUNT
--- expect
num 1
===
### name: link.comma-list-on-the-right-reading-both-binders
--- note
A side of the comparison that reads BOTH binders (through a `,` list or a `;` sequence) is not one-sided, so the join is evaluated per pair and no binder is unbound. JS and Lisp classified it as one-sided and raised a spurious E_UNDEF_VAR.
--- source
LINK(LIST(RECORD("k", 1)), LIST(RECORD("k", 1)), _1["k"] == (_2["k"], _1["k"])) .> COUNT
--- expect
num 1
===
### name: link.comma-list-on-the-right-general-path-agrees
--- note
A side of the comparison that reads BOTH binders (through a `,` list or a `;` sequence) is not one-sided, so the join is evaluated per pair and no binder is unbound. JS and Lisp classified it as one-sided and raised a spurious E_UNDEF_VAR.
--- source
LINK(LIST(RECORD("k", 1)), LIST(RECORD("k", 1)), _1["k"] == (_2["k"], _1["k"]) AND TRUE) .> COUNT
--- expect
num 1
===
### name: link.sequence-operand-reading-both-binders
--- note
A side of the comparison that reads BOTH binders (through a `,` list or a `;` sequence) is not one-sided, so the join is evaluated per pair and no binder is unbound. JS and Lisp classified it as one-sided and raised a spurious E_UNDEF_VAR. The sequence is the right binder's id, which equals the right side for every pair.
--- source
R = LIST(RECORD("id", 1)); S = LIST(RECORD("id", 2), RECORD("id", 3)); COUNT(LINK(R, S, A, B, (A["id"]; B["id"]) == B["id"]))
--- expect
num 2
===
### name: link.sequence-operand-ending-in-the-left-binder
--- note
A side of the comparison that reads BOTH binders (through a `,` list or a `;` sequence) is not one-sided, so the join is evaluated per pair and no binder is unbound. JS and Lisp classified it as one-sided and raised a spurious E_UNDEF_VAR. The value of the sequence is the last expression, the left id 1, never equal to 2 or 3.
--- source
R = LIST(RECORD("id", 1)); S = LIST(RECORD("id", 2), RECORD("id", 3)); COUNT(LINK(R, S, A, B, (B["id"]; A["id"]) == B["id"]))
--- expect
num 0
===
### name: link.count-of-comma-list-operand
--- note
A side of the comparison that reads BOTH binders (through a `,` list or a `;` sequence) is not one-sided, so the join is evaluated per pair and no binder is unbound. JS and Lisp classified it as one-sided and raised a spurious E_UNDEF_VAR.
--- source
R = LIST(RECORD("id", 1)); S = LIST(RECORD("id", 2), RECORD("id", 3)); COUNT(LINK(R, S, A, B, COUNT((A["id"], B["id"])) == B["id"]))
--- expect
num 1
===
### name: link.assignment-operand-on-both-sides
--- note
A side of the comparison that reads BOTH binders (through a `,` list or a `;` sequence) is not one-sided, so the join is evaluated per pair and no binder is unbound. JS and Lisp classified it as one-sided and raised a spurious E_UNDEF_VAR. An assignment inside the predicate reads the binder it names on both sides.
--- source
R = LIST(RECORD("id", 1)); S = LIST(RECORD("id", 2), RECORD("id", 3)); COUNT(LINK(R, S, A, B, (N = B["id"]) == B["id"]))
--- expect
num 2
===

### name: sort.record-orders-by-its-first-field
--- note
A value with children and no scalar of its own is ordered by what scalar context makes of it (SPEC 3.2: its first child, recursively), so a record sorts by its first field; found by the differential fuzz, where C++ and Go treated every record as a tie.
--- source
JOIN(MAP(LIST(RECORD("k", 3, "v", "c"), RECORD("k", 1, "v", "a"), RECORD("k", 2, "v", "b")) .> SORT(), _["v"]), ",")
--- expect
text "a,b,c"
===
### name: sort-desc.record-orders-by-its-first-field
--- note
A value with children and no scalar of its own is ordered by what scalar context makes of it (SPEC 3.2: its first child, recursively), so a record sorts by its first field; found by the differential fuzz, where C++ and Go treated every record as a tie.
--- source
JOIN(MAP(LIST(RECORD("k", 3, "v", "c"), RECORD("k", 1, "v", "a"), RECORD("k", 2, "v", "b")) .> SORT_DESC(), _["v"]), ",")
--- expect
text "c,b,a"
===
### name: sort-desc.record-ties-keep-input-order
--- note
Records with an equal first field tie (1 and 1.0 and "1" are one value): a descending sort keeps them in input order and reverses only the unequal ones.
--- source
JOIN(MAP(LIST(RECORD("k", 1, "v", "a"), RECORD("k", 2, "v", "b"), RECORD("k", 1.0, "v", "c"), RECORD("k", "1", "v", "d")) .> SORT_DESC(), _["v"]), ",")
--- expect
text "b,a,c,d"
===
### name: sort.record-ties-keep-input-order
--- note
The ascending twin of sort-desc.record-ties-keep-input-order.
--- source
JOIN(MAP(LIST(RECORD("k", 1, "v", "a"), RECORD("k", 2, "v", "b"), RECORD("k", 1.0, "v", "c"), RECORD("k", "1", "v", "d")) .> SORT(), _["v"]), ",")
--- expect
text "a,c,d,b"
===
### name: sort.record-with-text-first-field-sorts-as-text
--- note
The record's first field is text that is not a number, so it sorts bytewise among the other text, after every number.
--- source
JOIN(MAP(LIST(RECORD("k", "b", "v", "b"), RECORD("k", 5, "v", "n"), RECORD("k", "a", "v", "a")) .> SORT(), _["v"]), ",")
--- expect
text "n,a,b"
===
### name: sort.record-with-bool-first-field-ranks-as-bool
--- note
The record's first field is a BOOL, so it ranks as one: below numbers, FALSE before TRUE.
--- source
JOIN(MAP(LIST(RECORD("k", 5, "v", "n"), RECORD("k", TRUE, "v", "t"), RECORD("k", FALSE, "v", "f")) .> SORT(), _["v"]), ",")
--- expect
text "f,t,n"
===
### name: sort.nested-record-follows-the-first-child-down
--- note
Scalar context recurses: a record whose first child is a record is ordered by that record's first field.
--- source
JOIN(MAP(LIST(RECORD("k", RECORD("x", 2), "v", "b"), RECORD("k", RECORD("x", 1), "v", "a")) .> SORT(), _["v"]), ",")
--- expect
text "a,b"
===
### name: top-desc.record-ties-keep-input-order
--- note
TOP_DESC obeys the same tie rule as SORT_DESC.
--- source
JOIN(MAP(LIST(RECORD("k", 1, "v", "a"), RECORD("k", 2, "v", "b"), RECORD("k", 1, "v", "c")) .> TOP_DESC(3), _["v"]), ",")
--- expect
text "b,a,c"
===
### name: sort-by.desc-tied-keys-keep-input-order
--- note
SORT_BY with a descending direction keeps tied keys in input order (a key of 1 and one of 1.0 are equal).
--- source
JOIN(MAP(LIST(RECORD("k", 1, "v", "a"), RECORD("k", 2, "v", "b"), RECORD("k", 1.0, "v", "c")) .> SORT_BY(_["k"], "DESC"), _["v"]), ",")
--- expect
text "b,a,c"
===
### name: sort-by.desc-null-keys-tie
--- note
Keys that are NULL (a missing-key default via ??) are all equal, and rank below everything: descending puts the other keys first and keeps the NULLs in input order.
--- source
JOIN(MAP(LIST(RECORD("v", "a"), RECORD("k", 1, "v", "b"), RECORD("v", "c")) .> SORT_BY(_["k"] ?? NULL, "DESC"), _["v"]), ",")
--- expect
text "b,a,c"
===
### name: top-by.desc-ties-keep-input-order
--- note
TOP_BY with a descending direction keeps tied keys in input order.
--- source
JOIN(MAP(LIST(RECORD("k", 1, "v", "a"), RECORD("k", 2, "v", "b"), RECORD("k", 1, "v", "c")) .> TOP_BY(_["k"], "DESC", 3), _["v"]), ",")
--- expect
text "b,a,c"
===
